<p>
  A full bathroom remodel in Plano typically takes <strong>four to eight weeks</strong>, depending on the scope. Understanding the phases — and where the timeline usually slips — helps you plan around the project.
</p>

<h2>The phases of a Plano bathroom remodel</h2>
<h3>Weeks 1–2: Design and selections</h3>
<p>
  The bathroom is small enough that the design phase is quick, but tile, fixture, and vanity selections need to be finalized before construction starts. Locking these in early is the single best way to keep the project on schedule.
</p>
<h3>Weeks 2–3: Permits and prep</h3>
<p>
  Bathroom remodels that relocate plumbing or update electrical typically require a permit with the City of Plano. While the permit processes, we protect your home and order materials so construction can start the moment approvals are in hand.
</p>
<h3>Weeks 3–4: Demo, rough-in, and waterproofing</h3>
<p>
  The old bathroom comes out, new plumbing and electrical go in, and — most importantly — the shower walls and pan are waterproofed. In a bathroom, waterproofing is the step that cannot be rushed.
</p>
<h3>Weeks 5–7: Tile, vanity, and fixtures</h3>
<p>
  Tile is the longest construction step — typically three to seven days depending on the pattern and size. After that come the vanity and countertop, toilet, fixtures, lighting, and glass.
</p>
<h3>Final week: Finishing and punch list</h3>
<p>
  The last few days cover trim, caulking, final adjustments, and a detailed walkthrough. A clean finish here is what separates a remodel that ends smoothly from one that drags.
</p>

<h2>How the timeline varies by scope</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Full bathroom remodel:</strong> 4–6 weeks.</li>
  <li><strong>Master bath remodel:</strong> 6–8 weeks, with larger square footage and more tile.</li>
  <li><strong>High-end master baths:</strong> 8+ weeks when premium materials and extensive plumbing changes are involved.</li>
  <li><strong>Focused garden tub conversions:</strong> usually 2–4 weeks, because the work stays on the tub's existing footprint. See our <a href="/blog/garden-tub-to-walk-in-shower-plano/">garden tub conversion guide</a>.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What keeps a bathroom remodel on schedule</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Finalizing tile and fixtures before demo starts.</li>
  <li>Keeping the shower on existing plumbing where possible.</li>
  <li>Working with one team that handles design and construction together.</li>
</ul>

